course: dictionary
lesson: 1700
title: Words from church life
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to a particular place, and you learn their meaning by seeing how people use them there. A churchwarden is a volunteer who helps look after a church. «In the end he owned up to all of it, went to confession, said sorry to the churchwarden (a helper who looks after the church) and paid for a new hymn book from his pocket money.» The brackets in this sentence tell you the meaning. The churchwarden is a helper, not a teacher or a vicar.
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence. He said sorry to the churchwarden, which means the churchwarden is someone you can talk to about a problem at church. He paid for a new hymn book, so the churchwarden's job includes caring for things used in the church. Ask yourself what kind of person would do those jobs, and the answer is a helper.
explain: A churchwarden helps the vicar, the church leader, with the church building, its money and its services. In a village story, the churchwarden might welcome people at the door or help run the Sunday service. Here you see the word in its church sense, which is the one this lesson is about.
fact: A churchwarden is a volunteer in a parish who helps look after a church.
fact: A parish is a local area that has its own church.
fact: Churchwardens look after the church building, its money and its services.
fact: Churchwardens often help the vicar, who is the leader of the church.
fact: «Churchwarden» has an old meaning too: a long clay pipe for smoking.
exercise: «...said sorry to the churchwarden (a helper who looks after the church)...» Who is the churchwarden? | A teacher at the village school | A king who rules the land | *A helper who looks after the church | A boy who lost his pocket money
exercise: A churchwarden looks after which things? | Nothing, because churchwardens only sit and sing | Only the sea and the boats in the harbour | *The church building, its money and its services | The fields and the animals on the farm
exercise: Sam finds an old churchwarden in his grandad's shed. What is it? | *A long clay pipe for smoking | A helper who looks after a church | A new hymn book for the choir | A bell that rings on Sundays
test: A churchwarden often helps which church leader? | The lifeguard | The pilot | *The vicar | The baker
test: In the sentence, what do the words in brackets tell you? | What the boy ate for lunch | How old the hymn book is | *What a churchwarden is: a helper who looks after the church | Where the boy lives: in a big city
exam: Which sentence best describes a churchwarden? | *A volunteer who helps look after a parish church, its money and its services | A teacher who runs a school in the village | A shopkeeper who sells hymn books and sweets | A doctor who looks after people when they are ill
